Showcase_DemoModule_Title=Demo Module
Showcase_DemoModule_Body=A small self-contained scenario exercising MWS logic and expression capability beyond layouts — gated conditionals, random/shuffled draws, a checkpoint, input, foreach, and a real terminal passage. Not part of the layout catalog above; see Demo_Hub.mws.yaml.
Showcase_DemoModule_Link=Enter the Demo Module

Showcase_EarlyYears=Early Years
Showcase_MiddleYears=Middle Years
Showcase_LateYears=Late Years
Showcase_HandDevice=Carefully hand this storybook device to the reader and do not allow any other players to see the screen.

Showcase_NarrationShort_Title=A Quiet Morning
Showcase_NarrationShort_Body1=The fog lifted from Laurelton just after dawn, revealing rooftops still slick with the night's rain.
Showcase_NarrationShort_Body2=It was, by all accounts, an unremarkable start to what would become a very remarkable day.

Showcase_NarrationLong_Title=The Correspondence
Showcase_Letter_Arrival=The letter arrived on a Tuesday, though the postmark suggested it had wandered through at least three cities before finding its way to Laurelton. Its author, an old university colleague, wrote with the particular enthusiasm of someone who had not yet learned the cost of ambition.
Showcase_Letter_Recall="You will recall," the letter began, "our conversations in the amphitheatre, before the faculty grew tiresome of our methods. I write now not out of nostalgia, but out of necessity — I have found something in the old records that I believe belongs to your particular expertise, and to no one else's."
Showcase_Letter_Fragments=What followed was three pages of dense, cramped handwriting, describing a set of experiments abandoned decades prior by a researcher whose name had been carefully struck from every surviving record. The colleague had found only fragments: a single page of notation, a sketch of an apparatus no longer extant, and a warning, underlined twice, that the work should never be resumed by anyone lacking a very particular kind of patience.
Showcase_Letter_NotIgnored=It was, in other words, exactly the sort of correspondence that could not be ignored.
Showcase_Letter_Accustomed=The household had grown accustomed to such letters by now — invitations to obscurity dressed up as academic curiosity, forwarded from one estranged colleague to the next until they found a family willing to entertain them. Most were discarded within the hour. This one was not.
Showcase_Letter_Evening=By evening, the notation had been transcribed twice, cross-referenced against the family's own considerable library, and pinned to the wall of the study alongside a dozen other threads that had, over the years, refused to resolve into anything resembling an answer. Whether this one would prove different remained, as always, a matter for the coming weeks to decide.
Showcase_Letter_Cost=What could be said with some confidence was this: the letter had cost nothing to receive, and everything to answer.

Showcase_Introduction_Title=The First Generation
Showcase_Introduction_Body1=Every household begins somewhere. For this one, it began with a deed, a debt, and a great deal more ambition than sense.
Showcase_Introduction_Body2=What follows over the next three years will decide whether that ambition was warranted.

Showcase_Event_Title=A Bid for Mayor
Showcase_Event_Body1=All players take all their money into their hands. They then secretly choose any amount to place into their right hand to bid on becoming Mayor.
Showcase_Event_Body2=Once all players are ready, reveal the amounts simultaneously. The player that bid the most wins the election. (In the event of a tie, the tied player who went last in turn order the previous round wins the bid.)
Showcase_Event_Body3=Only the player that wins the election must pay their bid to supply. All others return their money to their Estates.

Showcase_Hub_Title=Laurelton
Showcase_Hub_Continue=Click here at the end of the round to continue...
Showcase_Hub_Hospital=The Hospital
Showcase_Hub_Library=The Library
Showcase_Hub_BuildHospital=Build a Hospital
